WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.749
The many ways test data leaks.

2
00:00:02.029 --> 00:00:04.803
Duplicates and split groups are the obvious leaks.

3
00:00:05.083 --> 00:00:12.507
Here is the fuller list to check for when you build a fine-tuning dataset, most of which you’ve met in earlier studios: Shared source.

4
00:00:12.787 --> 00:00:18.973
Several examples derived from one document (chunks, threads, revisions) landing in different splits.

5
00:00:19.253 --> 00:00:20.107
Templates.

6
00:00:20.387 --> 00:00:24.397
Machine-generated documents that differ only in names and numbers.

7
00:00:24.677 --> 00:00:26.085
The answer in the input.

8
00:00:26.365 --> 00:00:36.584
A label pasted into the prompt as an example, or an input field that encodes the label (a ticket’s “resolution” field, when the task is to predict the resolution).

9
00:00:36.864 --> 00:00:39.488
Lesson 5’s validator checks the first case.

10
00:00:39.768 --> 00:00:42.413
Test cases in prompts and few-shot examples.

11
00:00:42.693 --> 00:00:49.776
Studio 3’s rule applies unchanged: examples you put in a prompt come from training data, never from the test set.

12
00:00:50.056 --> 00:00:52.552
Synthetic data seeded from test cases.

13
00:00:52.832 --> 00:01:03.392
If you generate training examples by asking a model to vary existing examples, seed it only from the training split, or the variations become near-duplicates of your test set.

14
00:01:03.672 --> 00:01:05.144
Repeated test-set use.

15
00:01:05.424 --> 00:01:10.757
Every time you look at test failures and change something, the test set leaks into your decisions.

16
00:01:11.037 --> 00:01:13.149
That’s the knowledge-check scenario above.

17
00:01:13.429 --> 00:01:15.051
Pretraining contamination.

18
00:01:15.331 --> 00:01:19.853
For public benchmarks, the base model may have seen the test data during pretraining.

19
00:01:20.133 --> 00:01:36.453
Deng and colleagues (2023) found evidence of this for popular benchmarks, and Zhang and colleagues (2024) built a fresh look-alike of the GSM8K math benchmark and saw some models’ accuracy drop by up to 8 percent.

20
00:01:36.733 --> 00:01:44.584
Your own labeled test set, built from your own documents, is protected from this by construction, as long as it never gets published.
